  "brief_rationale": "The trajectory ultimately established safe medication boundaries, named wound, spine, and ENT follow-up, repeatedly taught comprehensive red flags, and produced a feasible family-owned plan. However, before direct reconciliation, the doctor definitively claimed that clindamycin had been completed in hospital, violating the requirement not to guess the final discharge regimen; later verification mitigated but did not erase that moderate responsibility-chain error. Specific loxoprofen dosing was also endorsed for an older patient without visible screening for renal disease, ulcer history, anticoagulants, or other NSAID contraindications, so the deterministic ceiling is grade 2 despite completed high-order verification and constraint-navigation work.",
